              "<strong>2. Private Nuisance (Pure Civil Tort):</strong><br/>• Unlawful and continuous interference with the use, enjoyment, or comfort of private land.<br/>• <em>Two Types of Private Nuisance:</em><br/>  (a) <em>Injury to Property:</em> Tangible physical damage to land or buildings (e.g., noxious fumes killing trees, vibrations cracking walls, *St. Helen's Smelting Co. v. Tipping*).<br/>  (b) <em>Injury to Comfort and Health:</em> Substantial interference with personal physical comfort (e.g., excessive industrial noise, foul odours, continuous barking of dogs).",
              "<strong>Criminal vs. Summary Removal of Public Nuisance:</strong><br/>Under Indian law, public nuisance is both an offence under Section 268 IPC and subject to summary removal under <strong>Section 133 of the Code of Criminal Procedure (CrPC)</strong>. A Sub-Divisional Magistrate or District Magistrate can pass urgent conditional orders to demolish hazardous structures, remove obstructions from public highways, or halt toxic emissions."
